  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the investigation:\n\n**Root cause location:** `lib/ansible/galaxy/collection/__init__.py`\n\n- `_extract_tar_dir(tar, dirname, b_dest)` looks up directory members via `tar._ansible_normalized_cache[dirname]` \u2014 a private, hand-built dict \u2014 instead of calling the standard `tarfile.TarFile.getmember()`. The cache is populated during `install_artifact`'s extraction pass by normalizing/stripping trailing path separators from member names, then attached as `tar._ansible_normalized_cache`. `_extract_tar_dir` itself also does `.removesuffix(os.path.sep)` on the lookup key before hitting that cache.\n- This creates a mismatch class of bugs: if the cache's key-normalization doesn't line up with how a given archive actually stored its directory member name (e.g., canonical form with no trailing separator vs. the cache expecting to strip one), lookups can miss or resolve the wrong entry \u2014 raising `AnsibleError('Unable to extract...')` or worse.\n- `concrete_artifact_manager.py` shows the pattern that *should* be used everywhere: plain `tar.getmember(MANIFEST_FILENAME)` / `collection_tar.getmember(...)`, which is what stdlib's `getmember()` already handles correctly on current Python.\n\n**Existing test scaffold:** `test/units/cli/galaxy/test_collection_extract_tar.py` already mocks `_ansible_normalized_cache` directly (bypassing real tarfile behavior), which is exactly the fragility being reported \u2014 it doesn't exercise a real `tarfile.TarFile` with standard member names, so it can't catch the lookup mismatch. `test/units/galaxy/test_collection_install.py` shows the project's convention for building real in-memory tarballs for install tests, useful as a template for a regression test that builds an actual tar archive and asserts extraction behavior against it.",
